A||N||14||Ronnie||HELP! Engine died||||1||
Z||000000||Ronnie||12-05-2000||04:25 PM||||Coming home today, my motor just died . I coasted into my driveway! Acts like it's not getting gas , turniing over but not hitting a lick. I checked all my fuses , they're ok. Help! Any ideas as to what to check? Could it be jumped timing? Control module? Any answers appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||12-05-2000||04:32 PM||||Start digging a grave  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Did the engine make a "pop" when it quit, I heard of ignition modules going out like that.  This a 4 cyl?  To check for gas, take the aircleaner off and have a friend crank the engine, look for gas coming out from the TBI.  Next would be to check the timing, if that seems ok, maybe look into the coil.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||12-05-2000||04:33 PM||||<BR>Question: Do you hear your fuel pump whir when you turn on the key?  It should whir for about 2 seconds.<P>Possible Fuel Filter?<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ronnie||12-05-2000||05:27 PM||||It's a 86 GT , V6. It sounded like it was not up to full power when I left work. But then it seemed to be running ok til it quit. Yes , I can hear the fuel pump.Don't remember hearing a pop, other than the sudden shutoff.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||12-05-2000||05:34 PM||||It's not the fuel filter. Check for spark and for fuel. My guess is spark.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||12-05-2000||05:36 PM||||Was the tach jumping around while it was still running? Check the ignition module in the distributor, and the coil. A shorted tach filter can cause it, too. <BR>BTW... Welcome to the forum!<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ronnie||12-05-2000||09:01 PM||||Will check it out tomorrow to see if I'm getting fire to the plugs. How do you check the ignition module?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||12-05-2000||11:37 PM||||I've heard that AutoZone was able to test modules. Don't know how reliable the test is, though. <BR>Before you go buying stuff, unplug the tach filter. It's a little round condensor-looking thing, under the coil, next to the head. It should have a white plug and wires. At least mine does. Just unplug that connector. The engine will run, but the tach will read zero. <BR>It may also be heat related. It's not unheard of for ignition parts to fail when they get hot, but seem to be okay when they cool off. Makes it a little more fun.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ed||12-06-2000||12:39 AM||||I vote for the ignition module.  My six was running PERFECTLY, then just quit.  Bad module.    <P>Crank engine, smell the exhaust for fuel.  Check a plug wire for spark.  Quick check for spark and fuel.  If you have fuel and no spark take out the module and have it checked at a parts house.  Easy to do.  If you need to replace, use GM parts (ONLY) because of the heat in the engine compartment. <P>Another possibility is that your engine just needs a tune-up.  I had my four fail the same way, but it was just a bad cap and rotor.  <P>Electronic engine control lets the engines run perfectly until they just quit all of a sudden.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ronnie||12-06-2000||05:12 PM||||Well , figured it out guys. Timing chain!!!<BR>The distributor is not turning while cranking the engine over. Thought it sounded like it was turning mighty easy. Oh well.<BR>thanks for all the suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||12-06-2000||05:58 PM||||<B><I>Owww!</I></B>||||reg||1
